rt/http/static/core/editing.js and core/hanging.js, and work.js imports openPanel from editing.js, so nothing is actually missing. The lint resolves the bare `core/...` spelling from the repo root. Scope: instance. Write the two references in chunk 04's Surfaces as full paths (`arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/editing.js`, `.../core/hanging.js`), as the chunk's own opening Surfaces line already does for work.js, so the lint resolves them.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ui-pass-work-artworks.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "themeChoice's 'a typed name that is already a theme joins it' only checks the themes it offers, so a held or shown theme's name is refused or duplicated", "recommendation": "core/membership.js themeChoice().chosen() looks for `taken` only in `offered`. The Work page passes the themes minus those already holding the work, and the selection passes the themes minus the shown theme. If a curator picks *New theme\u2026* and types the exact name of a filtered-out theme, the POST /api/themes hits the store's UNIQUE name and comes back as an error. If they type the same name in different case ('garden' when 'Garden' holds the work), a second, near-duplicate theme is made, although sameName() is case-insensitive by design. The docstring promises the opposite of both. Rule cited: 'When a safety check guards a filtered feature, range it over the population the hazard lives in'. Scope: class (the name check is scoped to the picker's filtered list). Fix: pass themeChoice every theme for the `taken` lookup and only filter what the picker shows. Then decide what joining a theme that already holds the work should say (e.g. the existing 'already' sentence). Add a Work-page test that types a held theme's name in another case and asserts from the server that no second theme exists.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/membership.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/selecting.js"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Restoring a work that is also kept off every wall moves focus to a hidden Hang\u2026, so the keyboard drops to <body>", "recommendation": "In paint(), focus 'primary' targets `page.hang || restoring || page.add`. Archiving does not clear a work's exclusion: only display.py's allow-again removes it, and archive_artwork lives in the library service. So after Restore on a work that was kept off, paintStrip has already set page.hang.hidden = true. document.contains(target) passes, focus() on a hidden button does nothing, and the replaced Restore button takes the keyboard with it. paint()'s own comment names this as the hazard it prevents. The base code focused the circulation control, which was always visible. Scope: instance. Pick the first control that is present and not hidden, e.g. `[page.hang, restoring, page.add].find((c) => c && !c.hidden)`. Pin it with a test that excludes, archives, then restores from the page and asserts which control has focus. refreshStrip's `focus && !focus.hidden` guard already handles this case on the strip path.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The held work's line of facts is unasserted: artist with (nationality, life), date, medium, size, and the mat's visible value", "recommendation": "workFacts() is new behaviour, including a mid-build decision to keep nationality and life in brackets after the artist. No browser test reads its text on a held work. test_the_page_opens_... checks only element order and that the mat trigger ends with '\u25be', and the Rothko tests click only its artist link. The rewritten mat tests (mat_fact) now assert the trigger's aria-label instead of the visible text the old summary assertions pinned, so the visible value (`shown`) is undefended too: a mutation that stops filling it, or that drops medium, dimensions or the bracket, keeps the suite green. Rule cited: 'When you drop or rewrite a test's assertion, replace a badly-shaped guard in the same commit'. Scope: instance. Add one exact-match assertion on `.work-facts` inner_text for a held work whose artist has a nationality and dates, plus a date, medium and dimensions. Also assert the trigger's visible text alongside its aria-label in mat_fact.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_work_page.py", "arrt/tests/browser/test_the_mat.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "note", "title": "Done-when 04.3 'Cancel or Escape in each dialog changes nothing' is not exercised for Restore's question or Archive's Escape", "recommendation": "These are covered: the walls dialog, Add to theme\u2026 and Match on Wikidata\u2026 (Cancel and Escape), and Archive\u2026 (Cancel, test_declining_the_question_leaves_the_work_where_it_was). Restore's confirmation has no decline test, and Archive's has no Escape test. Both go through the shared confirmAct, which is unchanged in this diff, so the risk is low. Scope: instance. Either parametrize the archive decline over Cancel and Escape and add a Restore decline asserting status from the service, or record in the plan that confirmAct's own tests cover these.", "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "Work page's filled act is decided by three signals in two places, so after Restore the keyboard can land on a hidden Hang\u2026", "recommendation": "instance. Which act fills the toolbar's first place depends on three signals: `work.status` (archived), `work.image.available`, and `where.placements.excluded_at` (kept off every wall). `paint()` creates `page.hang` from the first two only, `paintStrip()` hides it from the third, and `paint()`'s focus choice (`focus === \"primary\" ? page.hang || restoring || page.add`) ignores the third. This path can happen: the server's archive route does not clear an exclusion (`archive_work` in http/api.py, and `allow_work` is the only thing that removes one), so for a work kept off every wall, the sequence Archive\u2026 then Restore repaints with `where` carried over. `page.hang` exists but is hidden, `focus()` on it does nothing, and the keyboard drops to `<body>`, which is the failure the `focus` docstring says this code exists to prevent. `refreshStrip` already checks `!focus.hidden`, which shows the rule is known in one place and missing in the other. Fix: compute the primary act once from all three signals (for example a `primaryAct(work, placements)` that returns hang, restore or none) and use that one answer for both visibility and the focus target, falling back to *Add to theme\u2026*. Add a browser test for kept \u2192 archive \u2192 restore that asserts where focus ends up.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "`themeChoice` and `themeChoices` are near-homonyms in two modules, and the Work page imports the selection-mode module only for the mapper", "recommendation": "instance. `core/membership.js` exports `themeChoice` (the picker) and `core/selecting.js` exports `themeChoices` (the `GET /api/themes` \u2192 `[{theme_id, name}]` mapper). `screens/work.js` imports `selecting.js`, the list *Select* mode, only for that mapper. Moving `themeChoices` into `membership.js` beside its only consumer shape, and perhaps renaming it (`offeredThemes`), keeps the one-mapping guarantee and removes the near-collision.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/selecting.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/membership.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "'Close the dialog before moving focus' is repeated by every openPanel caller rather than owned by openPanel", "recommendation": "class. The platform `<dialog>` hands focus back to its opener on close, so every caller that moves the keyboard after an act must close the dialog first. This diff restates and reimplements that ordering at five sites: `getAndSay`'s `started` hook in core/getting.js, `finish` in core/selecting.js, and `addToTheme`, `chooseWall` and `matchOnWikidata` in screens/work.js. Each site is correct today. A sixth caller that forgets the ordering would have its focus taken from it with no error. To close the class, give `core/editing.js` the construction, for example `close({ thenFocus })` or a wrapper that runs an act and closes before focusing, so callers stop carrying the rule. Search: `openPanel(` call sites.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/getting.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/selecting.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "The page pattern 'one line of facts joined by \u00b7' is built three separate ways", "recommendation": "class. `design-direction.md` \u00a7 How a page reads makes the facts line a shared page shape, but the `\u00b7`-joined `p.muted.page-facts` is built inline twice in screens/work.js (`viewRegistryWork`'s `factLine` and `workFacts`, each with its own `flatMap` join) and as a string in screens/collection.js `factsText`. A small `pageFacts(parts)` in core/ (render.js or toolbar.js) would make the separator and class one decision before the next plans (Walls, Themes) add more pages. Search `page-facts` across `static/screens/`.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/collection.js"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Two comments in collection.js still say the heading carries the count and names the theme", "recommendation": "instance. The `h1` is now always 'Artworks', and the count and the narrowing moved to the facts line (`factsText`). The comment above `anyFacetChosen` ('the heading names it') and the comment before the fetch ('what makes the count in the heading a statement about the catalogue') now describe the old shape. Replace 'heading' with 'facts line' in both, or make them relational (point at `factsText`). Comment wording, nothing reads it, and no separate fix commit is wanted; fold it into the next commit.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/collection.js"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions of ui-pass-work-artworks, for the owner", "recommendation": "Scope: none. For the owner. Each decision is recorded in the plan's 'Decisions made mid-build' with what settled it. 02: the toolbar sticks only while Select is on (the plan's own fallback, taken up front). 02: Add to theme... became a dialog because photographs showed the inline picker wrapping to four rows. 02 to 03: Get's picker got the same treatment and split into chunk 03; the plan says 'found by building; the alignment pass did not settle it', which is the one alignment-pass miss. 02: on a phone, View, Sort and the filters leave the sticky toolbar while selecting. 02: the stale-theme note stays a notice. 03: both openers are quiet (one filled act per region). 04: themeChoice was extracted to core/membership.js so there is one answer to 'typed name that is already a theme'. 04: one wall is asked about directly, with no one-item dialog. 04: the mat opens inline because a modal would cover the preview. 04: the artist's nationality and life were kept. 04: Hang... leaves the toolbar while the work is kept off. The plan's one 'Inferred:' (the selection-acts ruling covers every list, not only Artworks) rests on design-direction.md's page rule 'A list acts on a selection. A table or grid of things has a Select mode; the acts on what is selected take the toolbar's left side', which binds as written. The operator-verification entry's 'The other lists' step gives the owner a direct look at it. Every new capability is used end to end: actionMenu by work.js, themeChoice by selecting.js and work.js, identityControl's open by Match on Wikidata. Goal 6: every new act goes through attempt() or hangSelection, so a refusal is said beside its control, and a failed mat read paints 'Unknown'. themeChoice's offered list grows only by themes the curator makes in that dialog. Nothing in Goal 6 needs action.", "files": ["arrt/src/arrt/http/static/screens/work.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/membership.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/selecting.js", "arrt/src/arrt/http/static/core/toolbar.js"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog item appears resolved: #389 curation-ui: Work and Artworks pages to the 2026-10-27 page rules", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Backlog item appears resolved: brookstalley/arrt#389 (status in-progress). This branch rebuilds Artworks and Work to the rulings. Verify it, then call `/prawduct:backlog update 389 status=shipped closed-by=scope:ui-pass-work-artworks` when the backlog skill's 'When to mark shipped' rule says so. The owner's walk (chunk 05) is still to come, so that may be after the release deploys.", "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "C-B3: open curation-ui item #94 sits beside the rebuilt Work page", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Open items in area curation-ui: #94 (per-work preview of the e-paper label card) concerns the Work page this branch rebuilt, and no chunk recorded an assessment of it. Assess it and update its status. It is most likely untouched and stays open, but say so. #390 is the named next plan and needs nothing.", "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "C-B4: #392, cited by the plan as the Walls plan's item, does not resolve in the backlog cache", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. build-plan-ui-pass-work-artworks.md 'What this plan is' cites '(build-plan-ui-pass-walls.md, #392)'. `cache-query resolve 392` reports no item. It is probably a closed issue or a PR number, which the open-item cache does not hold, rather than a typo. Confirm which it is. Nothing else is needed if it is the Walls PR or a shipped issue.", "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: no warned-against pattern reintroduced", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read list from `learnings-files --for-diff`: .claude/rules/learnings/core.md and .claude/rules/learnings/surface.md. Spot checks against the diff all held. 'When you drop or rewrite a test's assertion, ... record why': every rewritten h1 or bar assertion in test_the_collection.py and test_the_grid.py names ruling 1 or 8 of 2026-10-27. 'When a control is renamed for what it really does, align its styling too': Archive... carries no danger style. 'When a notice claims an ordering...': the toolbar and mat placement are held by position and bounding-box assertions. 'When a clause or notice is conditional, assert its absence': there are absence tests for no Rights, no Edit..., no Hang... on an archived work, and no kept-off line. No learnings rule was added or changed this cycle, and no implicit supersession was found.", "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "note", "title": "Records Pass: the oracle set clears no bar", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Covered set: .prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-ui-pass-work-artworks.md, .prawduct/artifacts/ia-proposal.md, .prawduct/artifacts/information-architecture.md, .prawduct/change-log.md, .prawduct/operator-verification.md and .prawduct/project-state.yaml. The change-log's claims match the code: actionMenu and themeChoice exist; Cancel and Escape tests exist for the walls dialog, Add to theme, Get and Match on Wikidata; the absence tests exist; no server file changed. The IA Work, Artworks, Search and Artists rows describe the built toolbar, dialogs and facts line. The operator-verification entry's